--TEST--
a `Core` parameter whose spec name is a reserved word is callable by that name — `fn:` in argument position is a name, and an anonymous function beside it is still a callable (`rule:core-api/shape-rules` R2)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `docs/spec/01-core-library.md` § 6 writes `Core\Arr::map(array<T> $a,
// callable $fn): array<U>`, so R2's "every parameter is callable by the spec's
// $name" makes `fn:` a name here. The `:` is the whole disambiguation: nothing
// else in argument position is a keyword followed by one, so the value on the
// same line can be an anonymous function without either reading being in doubt.
array<string> $shouted = Core\Arr::map(fn: fn (string $w): string => Core\Str::upper($w), a: ["ab", "cd"]);
echo Core\Str::join($shouted, "-"), "\n";

// All three of `reduce`'s names, none of them in the row's own order.
echo Core\Arr::reduce(initial: 100, fn: fn (int $acc, int $n): int => $acc + $n, a: [1, 2, 3]), "\n";

// A sibling member that names the same slot `$predicate` rather than `$fn`:
// one mechanism resolves both, and which word a row writes is the spec's.
array<string> $letters = ["a", "b", "c"];
echo Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::filter(predicate: fn (string $w): bool => $w != "b", a: $letters), ""), "\n";

// § 5's `replaceWith` names its third parameter `$fn` too, and its callback is
// an anonymous function written into the argument the name fills.
echo Core\Regex::replaceWith(fn: fn (Core\Regex\Match $m): string => "<" . $m->text() . ">",
    pattern: "\\d+", subject: "a1b22c"), "\n";
--EXPECT--
AB-CD
106
ac
a<1>b<22>c
